            <ArticleTitle>Rupture of the maternal renal calyx secondary to fetal malpresentation: a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>175-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Rupture of the renal collecting system is a potentially life-threatening condition in pregnancy. Most cases are associated with obstruction from nephrolithiasis or diseased renal parenchyma.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 24-year-old, nulliparous, African American woman at 38 weeks + 3 days' gestation presented with left flank pain refractory to conservative pain management. Computed tomography was negative for a stone but significant for infrarenal fluid and rupture of a left renal calyx. The fetal head was not flexed and appeared to be compressing the left ureter. A face presentation became apparent during her labor course, and she underwent a cesarean delivery when labor did not progress.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This represents the first reported case of a ruptured renal collecting system secondary to fetal malpresentation. A high index of suspicion is essential to diagnose collecting system rupture, and it may occur in the absence of parenchymal disease or stones.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
